WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.051 --> 00:00:03.267
(orchestra building)

00:00:04.927 --> 00:00:09.083
♫ It's the bitch of living

00:00:09.083 --> 00:00:12.891
♫ Living in your head ♫

00:00:12.891 --> 00:00:15.422
- Moritz Stiefel, I need hardly remind you

00:00:15.422 --> 00:00:18.209
that of all our pupils,
you are in no position

00:00:18.209 --> 00:00:20.205
to be taking liberties.

00:00:20.205 --> 00:00:22.445
I will not warn you again.

00:00:22.445 --> 00:00:26.025
("The Bitch of Living" by Duncan Sheik)

00:00:26.025 --> 00:00:27.612
♫ God I dreamed there was an angel

00:00:27.612 --> 00:00:30.445
♫ Who could hear me through the wall ♫

00:00:30.955 --> 00:00:32.883
- I depend on a lot of cues

00:00:32.883 --> 00:00:35.367
because I cannot hear a single thing.

00:00:35.367 --> 00:00:37.504
Not the tiniest little sound,

00:00:37.504 --> 00:00:40.595
no beats, no music, nothing.

00:00:43.355 --> 00:00:46.115
("Bitch of Living" by Duncan Sheik)

00:00:55.002 --> 00:00:56.322
♫ Nothing but your hand

00:00:56.322 --> 00:00:58.532
♫ Nothing but your hand

00:00:58.532 --> 00:01:00.397
♫ Just the bitch of living

00:01:00.397 --> 00:01:03.114
♫ As someone you can't stand ♫

00:01:03.114 --> 00:01:04.693
- We have several different kinds of cues

00:01:04.693 --> 00:01:06.225
that we use in the show.

00:01:06.225 --> 00:01:08.547
The main one, and the one that I prefer,

00:01:08.547 --> 00:01:10.080
are actor driven cues.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.447
And those are derived from normal looking,

00:01:13.447 --> 00:01:16.256
pedestrian movements
that a person would make,

00:01:16.256 --> 00:01:17.951
and that don't draw your eye.

00:01:17.951 --> 00:01:20.389
Like folding your arms, leaning forward,

00:01:20.389 --> 00:01:24.034
looking over at an
actor, simple body shift.

00:01:24.034 --> 00:01:27.262
It's, I think, the most complicated piece,

00:01:27.262 --> 00:01:28.701
and the part that the audience

00:01:28.701 --> 00:01:29.499
doesn't notice at all.

00:01:29.499 --> 00:01:30.646
- I've been doing this,

00:01:30.646 --> 00:01:31.472
(gasp)

00:01:31.472 --> 00:01:32.608
right before I sing the lyrics,

00:01:32.608 --> 00:01:34.181
so there's this kind of one second delay.

00:01:34.181 --> 00:01:35.133
So I'll lift my head,

00:01:35.133 --> 00:01:37.571
they know one second after
that to start their sign.

00:01:37.571 --> 00:01:39.545
- A tiny little thing
like nodding your head

00:01:39.545 --> 00:01:43.120
at the right time could really
screw up a whole number.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:46.882
♫ You wanna laugh, it's too absurd

00:01:46.882 --> 00:01:51.201
♫ You start to ask, can't hear a word

00:01:51.201 --> 00:01:53.430
♫ You wanna crash and burn

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:58.106
♫ Right, tell me more ♫

00:01:58.106 --> 00:02:00.953
- I have someone who's sitting
with his hand on the bed

00:02:00.953 --> 00:02:03.066
and when his right hand opens,

00:02:03.066 --> 00:02:05.434
and I see that, then I crumple the paper.

00:02:05.434 --> 00:02:08.034
Now, when another person stands up,

00:02:08.034 --> 00:02:11.401
that is my cue to start signing.

00:02:11.401 --> 00:02:13.282
And I guess it sort of goes on like that.

00:02:13.282 --> 00:02:15.999
- And each time Daniel takes a letter

00:02:15.999 --> 00:02:18.158
that's a cue that he's about to get ready.

00:02:18.158 --> 00:02:20.457
And then we have an actor
take the letter from him

00:02:20.457 --> 00:02:22.918
at the beginning of every
one of his musical phrases.

00:02:24.048 --> 00:02:27.956
♫ They're not my home, not anymore

00:02:27.956 --> 00:02:32.043
♫ Not like they so were before ♫

00:02:32.043 --> 00:02:33.854
- It creates a lot of
really interesting moments

00:02:33.854 --> 00:02:36.176
where, you know, on one side of the stage

00:02:36.176 --> 00:02:37.592
you'll see me singing the song,

00:02:37.592 --> 00:02:40.262
with Daniel, while he's signing the thing.

00:02:40.262 --> 00:02:42.733
And we just create this kind of

00:02:42.733 --> 00:02:46.160
really unique experience
that I've personally

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:48.528
never seen on a stage before.

00:02:48.528 --> 00:02:51.146
("Mama Who Bore Me" by Duncan Sheik)

00:02:56.353 --> 00:03:00.626
- Music is not an art form
that's typically available

00:03:00.626 --> 00:03:02.158
to the deaf community.

00:03:02.158 --> 00:03:04.921
So, in this production,
we're not just bringing

00:03:04.921 --> 00:03:07.498
sign language to hearing people,

00:03:07.498 --> 00:03:11.910
we're bringing musicals into
the deaf community as well.

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:14.185
So, to do that, we have to make music

00:03:14.185 --> 00:03:17.459
accessible to a deaf audience.

00:03:17.459 --> 00:03:19.804
So, one thing that we
work really hard to do

00:03:19.804 --> 00:03:22.007
is bring the emotion that's in the

00:03:22.007 --> 00:03:24.567
fabric of the musical and the story

00:03:24.567 --> 00:03:28.906
into the language that we
use to perform the music.

00:03:28.906 --> 00:03:32.459
- In the translation process,

00:03:32.459 --> 00:03:34.572
you've got to match the rhythm.

00:03:34.572 --> 00:03:37.381
The composer wrote a certain
rhythm into these lines,

00:03:37.381 --> 00:03:39.657
say, four beats to a line.

00:03:39.657 --> 00:03:42.559
How are we going to
find a sign translation

00:03:42.559 --> 00:03:45.136
that matches that rhythm smoothly?

00:03:45.136 --> 00:03:47.116
Is it a strong piece of music?

00:03:47.116 --> 00:03:48.944
Or is it soft?

00:03:48.944 --> 00:03:51.754
That's an important
part of the translation.

00:03:51.754 --> 00:03:55.283
As a deaf person, I've
never heard music before.

00:03:55.283 --> 00:03:58.441
So I depend on the hearing
members of our team.

00:03:58.441 --> 00:04:01.552
Once we understand from
them how the music goes,

00:04:01.552 --> 00:04:04.640
we try to find an equivalent.

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:07.473
- I've learned a lot through the process.

00:04:07.473 --> 00:04:08.913
It's an incredible challenge,

00:04:08.913 --> 00:04:12.047
probably the most challenging
role I've ever had,

00:04:12.047 --> 00:04:13.580
but it's been fantastic.

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:15.693
If another musical was offered to me,

00:04:15.693 --> 00:04:17.852
I mean, I'd take it immediately.

00:04:17.852 --> 00:04:21.451
I love music, it's really been wonderful.

00:04:21.451 --> 00:04:25.816
♫ Then there were none

00:04:25.816 --> 00:04:29.856
♫ Then there were none

00:04:29.856 --> 00:04:33.827
♫ Then there were none

00:04:33.827 --> 00:04:38.767
♫ Then there were none ♫

00:04:42.743 --> 00:04:45.454
(applause)

